# dryer Tool Spec (#148 / parent spec #146)

Purpose: structural duplication detection for Lua source functions. dryer (`qinyuanj/dryer`) replaces the retired dry4lua.

Upstream (adjacent checkout, ignored by git): `.uml-viewer/dryer`, cloned by hand next to the `crapper` / `mutator` checkouts (`.uml-viewer/` is already ignored). The repository is read-only from this project; fixes live upstream.
Project wrapper: `lua5.4 tools/cli.lua dry` (package `tools/packages/dry/`: `cli.lua` entry, `dryer.lua` pure judgement).

Setup (manual, same as crapper / mutator; this project never installs or updates it):
- `git clone https://github.com/qinyuanj/dryer.git .uml-viewer/dryer`
- Python 3.11+ (`requires-python >=3.11`; deps `tree-sitter>=0.24`, `tree-sitter-language-pack>=0.7`): `python3 -m venv .uml-viewer/dryer/.venv`, then `.uml-viewer/dryer/.venv/bin/python -m pip install -e .uml-viewer/dryer`.
- WSL Ubuntu smoke evidence (2026-10-21, dryer `0941c4b`): `.uml-viewer/dryer/.venv/bin/python -I -m dryer src` writes `.metrics/dry.edn`; the wrapper's own run is the record.

CLI contract:
- `lua5.4 tools/cli.lua dry [dryer options] [path-or-filter ...]`; every argument is forwarded to dryer unchanged (`--threshold N`, `--min-lines N`, `--min-nodes N`, `--root`, `-s/--source-root`, `--changed`, `--edn`, `--text`, ...). `--help` prints the wrapper contract.
- Exit codes follow `tools/cli.lua`: 0 effective result / 1 real failure / 2 usage error / 3 unavailable.
- The wrapper runs the venv interpreter directly (`.uml-viewer/dryer/.venv/bin/python -I -m dryer`, cwd = repo root). It does not depend on dryer's own launcher or its executable bit.

Preflight (check only, nothing is installed):
- Unavailable (exit 3, stderr `[dry] status=unavailable tool=dryer step=<checkout|venv>`, tool not started): `.uml-viewer/dryer/pyproject.toml` is missing (`checkout`) or the venv `bin/python` is missing (`venv`). It is an environment gap, not a finding; follow Setup and rerun.
- Real failure (exit 1, tool not started): the import probe fails, the venv Python is older than 3.11, or `dryer` imports from outside `.uml-viewer/dryer/src` (the same `python_verdict` as the crapper / mutator venvs, so a globally installed dryer cannot produce a false green).

Effective result (the anti-false-green rule):
- Before running, the wrapper moves the existing `<root>/.metrics/dry.edn` aside (`--root` selects the root; default is the repo root). After dryer exits, "the file exists" therefore means "dryer wrote it this run".
- Effective = dryer exit 0 AND candidate files > 0 AND a freshly written `.metrics/dry.edn`. dryer does not print a file count. With zero source files it prints `No source files to analyze.`, exits 0 and writes nothing, so a fresh file implies candidates > 0.
- Exit 1 naming the reason otherwise: nonzero dryer exit (code named), `No source files to analyze.` (candidate files = 0, "not 'no duplicates'"), or exit 0 without a new `.metrics/dry.edn`. A missing path is treated by dryer as a filter and lands in the zero-file case.
- On any failure the previous `.metrics/dry.edn` is restored byte for byte, so a failed run never loses the committed baseline. On success the old copy is discarded.
- Duplicates are advisory: `DUPLICATE score=...` rows (or none) never change the exit code. The soft-lane stance is unchanged.

Outputs and version control:
- dryer writes `.metrics/dry.edn` (`{:candidates [{:score :language :left :right :left-nodes :right-nodes} ...]}`), replaced on every successful analysis. It is versioned like the other `.metrics/` files: commit only files produced by a real `lua5.4 tools/cli.lua dry` run, never hand-written ones.
- Tests that scan fixtures pass `--root <temp dir>` so the committed file is never touched.

Scope notes from dryer's discovery (`discover.py`):
- Skipped directories include `test`, `tests`, `spec`, `specs`, `__tests__`, `vendor`, `target`, `build`, `.metrics` and others; `*_spec.lua` files are skipped. Explicitly named files bypass the test filter, which is how the fixture pair `tools/packages/dry/test/fixtures/duplicate_pair/ledger_{a,b}.lua` is scanned. The test filter looks at every path component, so a checkout living under a directory named `test` would find no files (reported as exit 1, never as success).
- Lua candidates are `function` declarations and function expressions assigned to a name; nested functions and unassigned closures are not compared. Default threshold 0.82.

Dry is a soft lane, never part of a hard gate: slim `verify` and CI do not run it. Tooling tests use injected process/filesystem doubles for the judgement, plus real-process cases that run only when the checkout and venv exist; without them the real-process suite asserts the exit 3 unavailable shape and nothing is reported as scanned.

Algorithm requirements (dryer):
- Parse source with tree-sitter, extract function scopes as normalized sub-trees, retain structural tokens (call/method names, operators, tree shape), drop local names, field names and literals.
- Compare structural fingerprints within one language by Jaccard similarity; report high-similarity pairs.
- Reports are advisory; refactor only when the duplication represents real shared behavior. Structural similarity is not semantic duplication.

Project workflow:
- Dry hits are discussion starting points, never tickets and never gates.
- Run before extracting a function (`lua5.4 tools/cli.lua dry <file-or-dir>`); sample at the default threshold before lowering it; below ~0.7 noise drowns real signals.
- Known duplication floor: `host_types` vec3/quat-style data classes are deliberately unmanaged; ignore hits there.
- Report format: command + target -> hit count + threshold -> top clusters worth extracting / ignoring -> next step.

Known gaps (upstream backlog, not implemented here):
- uml-viewer has no dry dispatch (`standardTools` / operation name) yet. Until it does, this project calls dryer's venv Python directly instead of the POSIX dispatch entry. Wanted upstream: a `dry` operation in uml-viewer.
- dryer prints no candidate file count; the wrapper infers "candidates > 0" from the fresh metrics file. Wanted upstream: print the count so the wrapper can report it.
